From 9ed9c57328fb7cea44cd808ad9c8b3a5ff10573f Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Jawad Amin <jawadamin@google.com>
Date: Thu, 10 Sep 2026 19:54:50 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 4/8] Mask padding rows out of the DeepSeek V4.1 cache writes

The TPU runner pads the token axis up to a compiled bucket. A padding row
inherits the last request's id, and its position points at a live cache
slot. The CUDA kernels get a slot_mapping of -1 for such a row, but the TPU
attention metadata does not carry slot_mapping, so the three JAX scatters
in the V4.1 path wrote the padding through.

Add _real_tokens, and mask on it in the compressor ring store, the
compressed KV insert, and the indexer key store.

Lift the ratio-2 pooling core into pool_ratio_two and the record packing
into build_kv_records. Both are pure functions of their arguments, so a
test drives the same code the layer runs.

Add numerical tests that compare each primitive against an independent
PyTorch reference of the CUDA kernel it replaces. The rotation reference
uses complex multiplication and the pooling reference walks one group at a
time, so a shared structure cannot hide a shared mistake.

Measured on a v6e-16 pod with JAX on CPU: 12 tests pass. Without the
_real_tokens mask, three padding rows corrupt 3072 of 16384 ring elements.
